# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "Single assignments", items 3 and 4.
id: assign-constant
title: Assignments whose weakest precondition is true
topic: Assignment
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "Single assignments" 3 and 4
tags: assignment, wp, true

task:
  Compute the weakest precondition of the program for the given
  postcondition, and decide whether the triple is valid.

pre: y = 2
post: x = 7 && x > y

program:
  {P}
  {R2}
  x := 7;          // i1
  {R1}
  x := y + 1       // i2
  {Q}

verdict: invalid

step: Look at the two parts of the exercise
text:
  The reference document asks for two separate triples:
  $\{true\}$ `x := 7` $\{x = 7\}$ and $\{y = 2\}$ `x := y + 1` $\{x > y\}$.
  Both are valid, and both have the weakest precondition $true$. We first
  solve them one by one. Then we look at what happens when the two
  assignments are put in sequence with the conjunction of the two
  postconditions, which is the program shown here.

step: The first triple, x := 7
rule: ASSIGN, WP
calc:
  wp(x := 7, x = 7)
  = (x = 7)[x <- 7]
  = 7 = 7
  = true
text:
  The formula $7 = 7$ is true in every state. So the triple
  $\{true\}$ `x := 7` $\{x = 7\}$ is valid: whatever the initial state, the
  assignment of a constant makes $x$ equal to that constant.

step: The second triple, x := y + 1
rule: ASSIGN, WP
calc:
  wp(x := y + 1, x > y)
  = (x > y)[x <- y + 1]
  = y + 1 > y
  = true
text:
  Again the weakest precondition is $true$, so $\{y = 2\}$ `x := y + 1`
  $\{x > y\}$ is valid, and the precondition $y = 2$ is not even needed.
vc: y = 2 ==> y + 1 > y
why:
  $y + 1 > y$ holds for every integer $y$.

step: The sequence, last statement first
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
calc:
  wp(x := y + 1, Q)
  = (x = 7 && x > y)[x <- y + 1]
  = y + 1 = 7 && y + 1 > y
  = y = 6
set: R1 = y = 6
text:
  The second conjunct is always true, so only $y + 1 = 7$ remains.

step: The first statement of the sequence
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
calc:
  wp(x := 7, R1)
  = (y = 6)[x <- 7]
  = y = 6           // x does not occur in the formula
set: R2 = y = 6
text:
  The assignment `x := 7` does not change $y$, so the formula does not
  change. The value $7$ given to $x$ is overwritten by the next statement
  anyway.

step: Compare with the precondition
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: y = 2 ==> y = 6
status: fails
counterexample: y = 2
why:
  In the state $y = 2$ the precondition holds and $y = 6$ does not.
  Running the program from that state gives $x = 3$, and $x = 7$ is false.
vc: {y = 2} x := 7; x := y + 1 {x = 7 && x > y}
status: fails
counterexample: y = 2, x = 0
result: Each original triple is valid, but their combination is not.

summary:
  An assignment of a constant has the weakest precondition $true$ for a
  postcondition about that constant. When another assignment to the same
  variable follows, the first value is lost, and the weakest precondition
  is computed from the last assignment.
